Z||000000||drednot||03-31-2001||05:18 PM||||WTF??  Why can't I get the pistons in the rear brake caliper to depress???  I have them all apart, and now I can't get them back together because I can't get the caliper on to the rotor!!!  What the hell???  all other cars that I have ever done, I just compresses the piston back in to the caliper with a c-clamp.  why won't this work here???  I am confused.  someone please help.  Thanks.||||reg||8
Z||000001||skitime||03-31-2001||05:28 PM||||You must turn the E-brake in. If you remove the e-brake lever you can turn it in with a wrench.<P>Good luck ||||reg||1
Z||000002||drednot||03-31-2001||05:39 PM||||that was the first thing that I did.  The e-brake is compleetly disconected.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||03-31-2001||05:42 PM||||Or you can rotate the piston.  Most auto parts stores sell a tool that fits into the two holes in the face of the piston.  You use a 3/8 drive to turn the tool.  It's the parking brake mechanism that's preventing you from just pushing the piston straight in.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000004||c m west||03-31-2001||05:44 PM||||no, the lever that turns the shaft which MANUALLY depresses the piston inside the caliper - remove the bracket, then watch the threaded shaft spin as you depress the caliper<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rodrv6||03-31-2001||05:44 PM||||Is the lever on the back of the caliper removed?  If so, the piston will push in without too much effort.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
